-- allium: 3
-- interaction-capture.allium
--
-- Browser extension that records a user's interaction with the open
-- web page and writes it down as a PiX interaction score
-- (a 3- or 5-layer "partitura" of User / Dialogue / System / ...).
--
-- This spec is the foundation for the new repository at ~/Sites/pix-extension.
-- It is a deliberate fork in intent from accesibilidad-inclusion/extension-chrome:
-- the upstream extension's reason for existing is to surface PICTOS aids from
-- pictos.cl. Here, the recording mechanism is repurposed so that what gets
-- captured is itself a PiX score, kept locally.
--
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Scope
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Included:
--   - Recording lifecycle (start, stop, single active score at a time).
--   - Capture granularity: one column appended to the score per user click
--     on an interactive element of the page.
--   - Screenshot of the visible viewport plus a normalised focus region
--     that highlights the clicked element.
--   - Mapping captured action -> PiX score step (column with one cell per
--     visible layer), seeded with a pixogram inferred from the element kind.
--   - Multi-site continuity: top-level navigations (including cross-origin
--     hops to a payment gateway and back) do not interrupt recording. The
--     ScoreStep that lands on a new host is tagged as a boundary crossing;
--     the active movement is never auto-split.
--   - Forensic posture: capture-derived fields (screenshot, captured_*,
--     boundary) are append-only once written. Per-Score provenance metadata
--     is recorded at creation time.
--   - Local-only score library (browser storage), survives browser restart.
--   - Export to two formats:
--       * a JSON document conforming to the PiX editor's score contract
--         (eadpucv/pix), with screenshots omitted and UUIDs preserved so
--         the user can enrich the score in PiX and re-import it.
--       * a step-by-step tutorial PDF (one page per captured ScoreStep,
--         viewport screenshot with the focus overlay above the
--         dialogue-cell text as the instruction caption).
--   - Re-import of a PiX-edited score back into the local library:
--     replace-in-place semantics that preserve capture-derived fields.
--
-- Excluded:
--   - All pictos.cl integration: aid-availability checks, in-page aid
--     overlay, sidepanel iframe to app.pictos.cl, "send task" upload,
--     and pictogram fetching from api.pictos.cl. This branch is offline.
--   - Browser-extension plumbing details (manifest shape, message bus,
--     service-worker lifetime). The browser is modelled as an external
--     boundary; specifics live in implementation, not in this spec.
--   - The PiX editor application itself (eadpucv/pix). This spec consumes
--     PiX's score data contract (layouts, layers, pixograms) as an
--     external vocabulary; that project owns it.
--   - Storage backend choice. The spec only commits to "local to the
--     browser profile, persistent across sessions, never on the network".
--     Implementation will use IndexedDB because screenshots immediately
--     exceed the Web Storage `localStorage` quota.
--   - Tamper-evidence beyond append-only semantics: no hash chains, no
--     digital signatures, no trusted timestamping. Provenance is recorded
--     but not cryptographically attested.
--   - Audit log of exports: exporting a Score does not mutate it.
--
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Dependency on eadpucv/pix
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The PiX-JSON re-import flow (UserImportsPiXScore) only delivers its
-- promise — preserving capture-derived fields by id-match through a
-- round trip — if the PiX editor preserves stable ids end-to-end.
-- As of the inspection that produced this spec, that is not the case
-- in eadpucv/pix:
--   * src/export/json.js strips the score's id; the export is just
--     { title, layout, description, scores }.
--   * Movements and ScoreSteps have no ids at all in PiX. Steps are
--     anonymous objects inside nested arrays (scores[i][j]).
-- For this spec's re-import to work as written, the PiX project must
-- be extended so that:
--   1. The exported JSON includes the score id.
--   2. Every Movement and ScoreStep is given a stable id at creation,
--      preserved through edit and round-trip.
-- Until that change ships, UserImportsPiXScore degrades to positional
-- matching, which silently misaligns capture-derived data when the
-- editor reorders or inserts steps. This is the most consequential
-- coupling between this repository and eadpucv/pix and the strongest
-- argument for co-locating both in one repository.

-- ============================================================
-- External Entities
-- ============================================================

-- The web page the user is interacting with. The extension observes
-- it; it does not own its lifecycle.
external entity WebPage {
    url: String
    viewport_width: Integer
    viewport_height: Integer
}

-- A DOM element on the current page that the recorder considers
-- worth capturing. "Worth capturing" is defined by the
-- ElementInteractivityCriteria invariant.
external entity InteractiveElement {
    page: WebPage
    tag_name: String                        -- button | a | input | select | textarea | ...
    role: String?                           -- ARIA role, if any
    text_content: String?                   -- visible text inside the element
    aria_label: String?
    placeholder: String?                    -- for inputs
    selected_option_text: String?           -- for <select> with a current selection
    bounding_box: BoundingBox               -- position and size within the viewport
}

-- A pixogram from the PiX icon vocabulary (160 monochrome SVG icons).
-- Identified by name only ("button", "input", "think", "process", ...).
-- The catalogue, layer suggestions and SVG sources are owned by the
-- eadpucv/pix project.
external entity Pixogram {
    name: String
}

-- A serialized PiX score, conforming to the data contract owned by
-- eadpucv/pix. Designed to open directly in the PiX web editor.
-- Screenshots are deliberately omitted (the user is expected to fill
-- the user layer in PiX, then re-import the enriched document back
-- into the extension). UUIDs of Score, Movement and ScoreStep travel
-- in the document so a re-import can match steps to their originals
-- and preserve the capture-derived fields the JSON does not carry.
external entity PiXScoreDocument {
    score_id: String                        -- target Score.id
    payload: String                         -- the JSON document, screenshots omitted
}

-- A step-by-step tutorial PDF generated from a Score. One page per
-- captured ScoreStep, showing the viewport screenshot with the focus
-- region painted as an overlay, plus the dialogue cell's text below
-- as the instruction caption. Intended to teach the reader how to
-- replicate the recorded sequence.
external entity TutorialPdf {
    score_id: String
    payload: Bytes
}

-- ============================================================
-- Value Types
-- ============================================================

value BoundingBox {
    x: Integer                              -- pixels from viewport left, of element centre
    y: Integer                              -- pixels from viewport top, of element centre
    width: Integer
    height: Integer
}

-- A single (layer x step) cell in a score. PiX renders cells as
-- "<pixogram> <text>". An absent cell has icon = null and text = "".
value Cell {
    icon: Pixogram?
    text: String
}

-- The highlighted region drawn over the screenshot in the editor.
-- Stored as percentages of the screenshot dimensions so the highlight
-- survives image resizing.
value FocusRegion {
    x_percent: Decimal                      -- 0..100, centre x as % of width
    y_percent: Decimal                      -- 0..100, centre y as % of height
    radius_percent: Decimal                 -- 0..100, as % of half the longest edge
}

-- Provenance metadata captured once at Score creation. Lets a reader
-- of the score (or a future investigator) know which browser/profile
-- produced it. Not cryptographically attested; the spec deliberately
-- stops short of signatures and trusted timestamping.
value ScoreProvenance {
    user_agent: String                      -- navigator.userAgent at create time
    extension_version: String               -- semver of the extension build
    timezone: String                        -- IANA name, e.g. "America/Santiago"
    locale: String                          -- BCP-47 tag, e.g. "es-CL"
}

-- ============================================================
-- Enumerations
-- ============================================================

-- The five PiX layers. A 3-layer score (layout = pix) uses
-- {user, dialogue, system}; a 5-layer Service Blueprint adds
-- environment and supporting_processes.
enum ScoreLayer {
    user | dialogue | system | environment | supporting_processes
}

enum ScoreLayout {
    pix                                     -- 3 layers: user, dialogue, system
    | sb                                    -- 5 layers: + environment, supporting_processes
}

-- How the recorder classified the clicked element. Drives the auto-title
-- and the seed pixogram in the dialogue cell.
enum CapturedElementKind {
    button | link | text_input | password_input | checkbox | radio
    | dropdown | text_area | generic
}

-- Marks a ScoreStep that occurred on a different host than the
-- previous step in its movement. The host is the eTLD+1 of the
-- captured_from_url. This is the multi-site forensic flag: the
-- canonical case is the user clicking through to a payment gateway
-- and later returning, all within one continuous recording.
--
-- The movement is never auto-split on a crossing; the marker is
-- metadata on the landing step. Crossings can chain without limit
-- (tienda -> gateway -> bank-3DS -> gateway -> tienda -> confirmation
-- is a single movement with several `crossing` steps).
enum BoundaryMarker {
    none                                    -- same host as previous step, or first step in movement
    | crossing                              -- this step's host differs from the previous step's host
}

-- ============================================================
-- Entities and Variants
-- ============================================================

-- The single global recording state for the extension. There is one
-- recorder per browser profile; toggling it on starts appending captures
-- to the currently active score.
entity Recorder {
    state: idle | recording
    active_score: Score?

    transitions state {
        idle -> recording
        recording -> idle
    }
}

-- A PiX score. Owned and edited locally; never uploaded anywhere.
--
-- The `id` is a stable UUID generated at creation. It travels in the
-- PiX-JSON export and is the key on which a re-import is matched
-- against the local library, so capture-derived fields (screenshots,
-- captured_*) survive a round-trip through the PiX editor that
-- never sees them.
entity Score {
    id: UUID                                -- stable across export/re-import
    title: String
    description: String
    layout: ScoreLayout
    movements: List<Movement> with score = this
    provenance: ScoreProvenance             -- captured once, at create time
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp

    -- Layers actually displayed in the score grid (depends on layout).
    visible_layers:
        if layout = pix:
            { user, dialogue, system }
        else:
            { environment, user, dialogue, system, supporting_processes }

    -- The movement currently receiving captures. A new score starts
    -- with one empty movement; it stays current until the user
    -- explicitly starts a new one.
    active_movement: movements.last

    total_steps: movements.sum(m => m.steps.count)
}

-- A horizontal sub-flow within a score. Multiple movements let a single
-- score document several distinct paths (happy + alternative + error).
entity Movement {
    id: UUID                                -- stable across export/re-import
    score: Score
    position: Integer                       -- index within score.movements (0-based)
    steps: List<ScoreStep> with movement = this
}

-- One column of the PiX grid: the cells that are simultaneously true
-- at one moment of the interaction.
--
-- Cells for layers not visible at the score's layout are still present
-- (so toggling layout preserves data) but are not edited or rendered;
-- see CellLayerCompatibility.
--
-- Capture-derived fields are populated when the step originated from a
-- click, and absent when the user inserted a step manually in the editor
-- (or when re-import created the step from a PiX-JSON document that
-- carried no capture data).
--
-- Capture-derived fields are append-only: once non-null, they are never
-- modified by any rule in this spec. See CaptureDerivedFieldsImmutable.
entity ScoreStep {
    id: UUID                                -- stable across export/re-import
    movement: Movement
    position: Integer                       -- index within movement.steps (0-based)
    step_title: String                      -- optional column header (above the grid)
    note: String                            -- optional caption (below the grid)

    user_cell: Cell
    dialogue_cell: Cell
    system_cell: Cell
    environment_cell: Cell
    supporting_cell: Cell

    captured_at: Timestamp?
    captured_from_url: String?
    screenshot: String?                     -- data URL of the viewport at click time
    focus: FocusRegion?
    captured_kind: CapturedElementKind?
    boundary: BoundaryMarker                -- defaults to none; see BoundaryReflectsHostChange
}

-- ============================================================
-- Config
-- ============================================================

config {
    -- Pixogram names used to seed the dialogue cell when a click is
    -- captured. The names match entries in the PiX icon catalogue.
    icon_button: String = "button"
    icon_link: String = "link"
    icon_text_input: String = "input"
    icon_password_input: String = "password"
    icon_checkbox: String = "checkbox"
    icon_radio: String = "radioselect"
    icon_dropdown: String = "select"
    icon_text_area: String = "text"
    icon_generic_click: String = "click"

    -- Pixogram seeded into the system cell when the URL changed since
    -- the previous captured step (i.e. a navigation occurred).
    icon_navigation: String = "page"

    -- Maximum number of scores held in the local library. Mirrors the
    -- PiX upstream ceiling; eviction policy on overflow is an open
    -- question (see below).
    score_library_capacity: Integer = 20

    -- Debounce window for re-scanning interactive elements after DOM
    -- mutations. Below this, mutation bursts are coalesced.
    interactive_rescan_debounce: Duration = 300.milliseconds
}

-- ============================================================
-- Defaults
-- ============================================================

-- The empty cell, used as the initial value for every cell in a new
-- step the user has not yet edited.
default Cell empty_cell = { icon: null, text: "" }

-- ============================================================
-- Rules
-- ============================================================

rule UserCreatesScore {
    when: UserCreatesScore(layout, title?)

    requires: Score.count < config.score_library_capacity

    ensures:
        let score = Score.created(
            id: new_uuid(),
            title: title ?? "",
            description: "",
            layout: layout,
            provenance: capture_provenance(),
            created_at: now,
            updated_at: now
        )
        Movement.created(
            id: new_uuid(),
            score: score,
            position: 0
        )
}

rule UserStartsRecording {
    when: UserStartsRecording(score)

    requires: Recorder.state = idle

    ensures: Recorder.state = recording
    ensures: Recorder.active_score = score
}

rule UserStopsRecording {
    when: UserStopsRecording

    requires: Recorder.state = recording

    ensures: Recorder.state = idle
    ensures: Recorder.active_score = null
}

-- The heart of the spec: one click on an interactive element while
-- recording produces exactly one new column in the active movement of
-- the active score. Hovers, scrolls and key presses do not capture.
--
-- Recording is resilient to top-level navigations: a click on an
-- element in a tab where the page navigated to a different URL (same
-- host or cross-origin, e.g. a payment gateway) still appends a step
-- to the same active movement. When the host of the click differs
-- from the host of the previous step in the same movement, the new
-- step is tagged boundary = crossing.
--
-- The recorder MAY seed the user_cell with a heuristic suggestion
-- (suggest_user_cell): for example, a "think" face when landing on
-- a form, a "happy" face after a confirmation page. The suggestion
-- is a hint for the editor, not a forensic claim about the user's
-- inner state; the user remains free to overwrite it. When no good
-- suggestion is available, suggest_user_cell returns empty_cell.
-- The exact heuristic is intentionally left to implementation; see
-- the open questions.
rule UserClickCapturesStep {
    when: UserClicksElement(element)

    requires: Recorder.state = recording
    requires: exists Recorder.active_score

    let score = Recorder.active_score
    let movement = score.active_movement
    let kind = classify_element(element)
    let dialogue_icon_name = pixogram_for_kind(kind)
    let dialogue_text = auto_caption(element, kind)

    let prev = movement.steps.last
    let url_changed = prev = null or prev.captured_from_url != element.page.url
    let host_changed = prev != null
        and host_of(prev.captured_from_url) != host_of(element.page.url)
    let user_seed = suggest_user_cell(element, kind, prev)

    ensures:
        ScoreStep.created(
            id: new_uuid(),
            movement: movement,
            position: movement.steps.count,
            step_title: "",
            note: "",
            user_cell: user_seed,
            dialogue_cell: {
                icon: Pixogram{name: dialogue_icon_name},
                text: dialogue_text
            },
            system_cell:
                if url_changed:
                    {
                        icon: Pixogram{name: config.icon_navigation},
                        text: element.page.url
                    }
                else:
                    empty_cell,
            environment_cell: empty_cell,
            supporting_cell: empty_cell,
            captured_at: now,
            captured_from_url: element.page.url,
            screenshot: capture_visible_viewport(element.page),
            focus: focus_from_box(element.bounding_box, element.page),
            captured_kind: kind,
            boundary: if host_changed: crossing else: none
        )
        score.updated_at = now
}

rule UserStartsNewMovement {
    when: UserStartsNewMovement(score)

    requires: Recorder.state = recording
    requires: Recorder.active_score = score

    ensures:
        Movement.created(
            id: new_uuid(),
            score: score,
            position: score.movements.count
        )
        score.updated_at = now
}

-- Manual editor actions on an existing score (no click capture involved).

rule UserAddsBlankStep {
    when: UserAddsBlankStep(movement)

    ensures:
        ScoreStep.created(
            id: new_uuid(),
            movement: movement,
            position: movement.steps.count,
            step_title: "",
            note: "",
            user_cell: empty_cell,
            dialogue_cell: empty_cell,
            system_cell: empty_cell,
            environment_cell: empty_cell,
            supporting_cell: empty_cell,
            captured_at: null,
            captured_from_url: null,
            screenshot: null,
            focus: null,
            captured_kind: null,
            boundary: none
        )
        movement.score.updated_at = now
}

rule UserEditsCell {
    when: UserEditsCell(step, layer, new_value)

    ensures:
        if layer = user:                step.user_cell = new_value
        else if layer = dialogue:       step.dialogue_cell = new_value
        else if layer = system:         step.system_cell = new_value
        else if layer = environment:    step.environment_cell = new_value
        else:                           step.supporting_cell = new_value
        step.movement.score.updated_at = now
}

rule UserEditsScoreMetadata {
    when: UserEditsScoreMetadata(score, title?, description?, layout?)

    ensures:
        if exists title:        score.title = title
        if exists description:  score.description = description
        if exists layout:       score.layout = layout
        score.updated_at = now
}

rule UserDeletesStep {
    when: UserDeletesStep(step)

    ensures: not exists step
    ensures: step.movement.score.updated_at = now
}

rule UserDeletesScore {
    when: UserDeletesScore(score)

    -- Cannot delete the score the recorder is currently writing into.
    requires: Recorder.active_score != score

    ensures: not exists score
}

-- Export and re-import.
--
-- Exports are explicit user actions on a saved Score. Neither export
-- mutates the Score: there is no audit log, no exported_at timestamp,
-- no hash trail. The forensic posture of this spec is append-only
-- capture (CaptureDerivedFieldsImmutable) plus per-Score provenance,
-- not export-side attestation.

rule UserExportsScoreAsPiXJson {
    when: UserExportsScoreAsPiXJson(score)

    -- Emits a JSON document conforming to the PiX editor's score
    -- contract (eadpucv/pix), suitable for opening directly in the
    -- PiX web app. Screenshots are deliberately stripped: PiX has no
    -- use for them, and the user is expected to enrich the user
    -- layer there before re-importing. The id, movement ids and
    -- step ids travel with the document so the re-import can match.

    ensures:
        PiXScoreDocument.emitted(
            score_id: score.id,
            payload: pix_json_of(
                score: score,
                with_screenshots: false,
                with_uuids: true
            )
        )
}

rule UserExportsScoreAsPdf {
    when: UserExportsScoreAsPdf(score)

    -- Emits a step-by-step tutorial PDF: one page per ScoreStep, in
    -- (movement, position) order. A step with a screenshot becomes a
    -- visual page (viewport image with the focus region painted on
    -- top, dialogue-cell text below as the instruction caption). A
    -- step without a screenshot — manually inserted in the editor or
    -- created by re-import from PiX — becomes a text-only page using
    -- step_title, dialogue_cell.text and note. No step is silently
    -- omitted: the PDF is the full forensic record of what the
    -- author recorded and chose to keep, in the order they chose
    -- to keep it.

    ensures:
        TutorialPdf.emitted(
            score_id: score.id,
            payload: pdf_tutorial_of(
                score: score,
                page_per_step: true,
                visual_page_when: "step.screenshot != null",
                text_only_page_when: "step.screenshot = null",
                instruction_text_source: "dialogue_cell.text"
            )
        )
}

-- Re-import of a Score the user enriched in the PiX editor.
--
-- Re-import is always invoked from the context of an existing local
-- Score (the target the user is currently viewing in the manager).
-- It is not a free-floating "open any PiX-JSON" action: the extension
-- treats foreign documents (whose id does not match the target) as
-- an explicit user error, not an opportunity to silently create a
-- new Score from data with no provenance and no screenshots.
--
-- Replace-in-place semantics: target_score has its title, description,
-- layout and editable fields overwritten from the document. Capture-
-- derived fields (screenshot, focus, captured_at, captured_from_url,
-- captured_kind, boundary) are never touched by re-import; they
-- survive because this spec never mutates them once written
-- (CaptureDerivedFieldsImmutable).
--
-- Reconciliation by stable id at every level (Score, Movement, ScoreStep):
--   * id known locally -> editable fields replaced from the document
--   * id new in the document -> created locally; capture-derived fields
--     are null (the document never carried them)
--   * id present locally but absent from the document -> deleted locally
--
-- The re-import always wins on conflict: if the user kept editing the
-- local Score after exporting, those edits are overwritten. This is
-- the agreed single-user, single-device model.
rule UserImportsPiXScore {
    when: UserImportsPiXScore(target_score, document)

    requires: target_score.id = document.score_id

    ensures:
        ReconcilePiXDocument.applied(
            target: target_score,
            document: document,
            policy: replace_in_place_preserve_captured
        )
        target_score.updated_at = now
}

-- ============================================================
-- Invariants
-- ============================================================

@invariant ElementInteractivityCriteria
    -- An InteractiveElement is one for which any of the following holds:
    --   1. tag_name in {button, a, input, select, textarea}
    --   2. ARIA role in {button, link, checkbox, radio, menuitem, tab,
    --      listbox, option, switch, searchbox, textbox, combobox}
    --   3. has an onclick attribute, or is an `a` with an href
    --   4. has tabindex other than -1
    --   5. has contenteditable = "true"
    -- Open and closed shadow roots are traversed. The set is rebuilt
    -- on DOM mutations affecting childList or the attributes
    -- {onclick, tabindex, role}, debounced by
    -- config.interactive_rescan_debounce.

@invariant CaptureGranularity
    -- Exactly one ScoreStep is appended per accepted UserClickCapturesStep
    -- firing. Hovers, scrolls, individual key presses on inputs and
    -- programmatic events do not produce steps. A click on a
    -- non-interactive element does not produce a step. A click while
    -- recording = false does not produce a step.

@invariant ScreenshotScope
    -- A captured screenshot is the visible viewport of the active tab
    -- at click time, encoded as a JPEG data URL. Off-screen page
    -- content is not captured. The focus region marks the element's
    -- bounding box at click time, normalised to percentages so the
    -- overlay tracks the image as it is rescaled.

invariant SingleActiveScore {
    Recorder.state = recording implies exists Recorder.active_score
    Recorder.state = idle implies Recorder.active_score = null
}

invariant ScoreLibraryCapacity {
    Score.count <= config.score_library_capacity
}

invariant CellLayerCompatibility {
    -- For the 3-layer pix layout, the SB-only cells are kept empty so
    -- that switching layout to sb later does not surface stale content.
    for s in Scores where layout = pix:
        for m in s.movements:
            for st in m.steps:
                st.environment_cell = empty_cell
                st.supporting_cell = empty_cell
}

invariant StepPositionsAreContiguous {
    for m in Movements:
        for st in m.steps:
            st.position >= 0 and st.position < m.steps.count
}

@invariant CaptureDerivedFieldsImmutable
    -- Once a ScoreStep has been written with non-null capture-derived
    -- fields (captured_at, captured_from_url, screenshot, focus,
    -- captured_kind, boundary), no rule modifies any of those fields.
    -- Editing dialogue_cell, user_cell, system_cell, environment_cell,
    -- supporting_cell, step_title or note is allowed and does not
    -- count as modifying the capture. The whole step can still be
    -- deleted (UserDeletesStep) and the whole score can still be
    -- deleted (UserDeletesScore) — append-only is per-field, not
    -- per-entity. Re-import (UserImportsPiXScore) follows the same
    -- rule: it never carries capture-derived fields and never
    -- overwrites the local ones.

@invariant RecordingSurvivesNavigationAndTabs
    -- Top-level navigations in the tab being recorded — including
    -- cross-origin hops to a payment gateway, redirects through a
    -- bank's 3DS flow, and the eventual return to the original
    -- origin — do not change Recorder.state. While Recorder.state =
    -- recording, every UserClicksElement firing on any host appends
    -- a step to Recorder.active_score.active_movement. Only an
    -- explicit UserStopsRecording transitions the recorder back to
    -- idle. The number of host crossings within a single movement
    -- is unbounded.
    --
    -- Recording also follows the user across tabs. When a click on a
    -- recorded page opens a new tab (target=_blank, window.open,
    -- middle-click, "open in new tab" from the context menu), the
    -- recorder begins capturing in that new tab against the same
    -- Recorder.active_score, with the first captured step there
    -- carrying boundary = crossing iff the host differs from the
    -- previous step's host. Closing the new tab and returning to
    -- the original tab continues capturing there, again subject to
    -- BoundaryReflectsHostChange. The "red light" indicator
    -- (RecordingStateAlwaysVisible) is shown in every tab where the
    -- recorder might capture, so the user is never unaware that
    -- they are being recorded in a tab they did not start the
    -- recording in.

invariant BoundaryReflectsHostChange {
    -- A step's boundary is `crossing` exactly when its captured_from_url
    -- is on a different host than the previous step's captured_from_url
    -- in the same movement. The first step of a movement, and any step
    -- whose host equals the previous step's host, has boundary = none.
    -- Manually-added steps (no captured_from_url) carry boundary = none.
    for m in Movements:
        for st in m.steps where st.captured_from_url != null:
            let prev = m.steps
                .where(s => s.position < st.position and s.captured_from_url != null)
                .last
            st.boundary = crossing iff (
                prev != null
                and host_of(prev.captured_from_url) != host_of(st.captured_from_url)
            )
}

invariant StableIdsAreUnique {
    -- Score, Movement and ScoreStep ids are globally unique within
    -- the local library. Re-import never collides because matching
    -- is by id and unmatched-incoming-ids create new entities.
    for s1, s2 in Scores where s1 != s2:        s1.id != s2.id
    for m1, m2 in Movements where m1 != m2:     m1.id != m2.id
    for st1, st2 in ScoreSteps where st1 != st2: st1.id != st2.id
}

-- ============================================================
-- Surfaces
-- ============================================================

-- The browser-extension boundary. Modelled abstractly: this spec does
-- not commit to Chrome MV3, Firefox WebExtensions or any specific API.
-- The deliberate guarantee here is that no network call ever leaves
-- the extension's process.
surface BrowserHost {
    facing browser: BrowserExtensionRuntime

    exposes:
        UserCreatesScore(layout: ScoreLayout, title: String?)
        UserStartsRecording(score: Score)
        UserStopsRecording
        UserStartsNewMovement(score: Score)
        UserAddsBlankStep(movement: Movement)
        UserEditsCell(step: ScoreStep, layer: ScoreLayer, new_value: Cell)
        UserEditsScoreMetadata(score: Score, title: String?, description: String?, layout: ScoreLayout?)
        UserDeletesStep(step: ScoreStep)
        UserDeletesScore(score: Score)
        UserClicksElement(element: InteractiveElement)
        UserExportsScoreAsPiXJson(score: Score)
        UserExportsScoreAsPdf(score: Score)
        UserImportsPiXScore(document: PiXScoreDocument)

    @guarantee NoNetworkSideEffects
        -- No rule in this spec sends data to a remote service. The
        -- recorder, score library and editor live entirely inside
        -- browser-local storage. This is the deliberate point of
        -- divergence from the upstream pictos.cl-integrated extension.
        -- Export rules emit artifacts (PiXScoreDocument, TutorialPdf)
        -- to the user's filesystem via the browser's download
        -- mechanism; that path does not count as a network call.

    @guarantee LocalOnlyPersistence
        -- Scores persist in browser-local storage. The spec commits
        -- to "local to the browser profile, persistent across browser
        -- restarts, never on the network"; the implementation backs
        -- this with IndexedDB (the same library pattern PiX upstream
        -- uses) because screenshots immediately exceed the Web Storage
        -- `localStorage` quota of ~5–10 MB per origin. Scores are not
        -- synced across devices and are not exposed to other tabs or
        -- extensions.

    @guarantee RecordingStateAlwaysVisible
        -- Whenever Recorder.state = recording, the user sees a
        -- persistent visual indicator that recording is active —
        -- the "red light" of a video camera. The indicator is
        -- visible on every tab where the recorder might capture
        -- (not only the tab where recording was started), so that
        -- the multi-site auto-continuation specified by
        -- RecordingSurvivesNavigation is never invisible to the
        -- user. The indicator disappears only after a successful
        -- UserStopsRecording.

    @guarantee CrossSiteCaptureScope
        -- The boundary marker on a ScoreStep is metadata only: it
        -- describes that the user crossed hosts since the previous
        -- step. It does not enable broader capture on the new host:
        -- screenshots are still viewport-only, capture is still
        -- click-only, and the recorder still only sees what the
        -- browser surfaces to a content script on the new page.
        -- Pages that block content-script injection (the browser's
        -- own internal pages, certain bank-side iframes) cannot be
        -- captured; recording on them is silent (no step is appended
        -- and no synthetic placeholder is emitted). The boundary of
        -- the next captured step is decided purely by host comparison
        -- with the previous captured step (BoundaryReflectsHostChange);
        -- the existence of skipped, uncapturable pages between them
        -- is not visible in the score. This is the agreed forensic
        -- posture: the score documents what was capturable, no more.

    @guarantee ImportRejectionExplained
        -- When UserImportsPiXScore(target_score, document) is rejected
        -- because target_score.id != document.score_id, the surface
        -- shows the user (a) that the document does not correspond to
        -- the Score they are editing, (b) that re-importing a foreign
        -- document would lose the connection that lets capture-derived
        -- fields (screenshots, focus regions) survive the round-trip,
        -- and (c) offers a one-click recovery: re-export target_score
        -- as PiX-JSON so the user can take the correct base partitura
        -- to the external editor, edit it there, and bring it back
        -- without breaking the id chain. The extension never silently
        -- creates a new Score from a foreign document.
}

-- ============================================================
-- Open Questions
-- ============================================================

open question "Library overflow at config.score_library_capacity: refuse new scores, evict the oldest, or warn the user? PiX upstream has the same ceiling and the question is open there too."

open question "Input-field granularity: today only the click that submits a form produces a step; intermediate keystrokes are not captured. Should the recorder also emit a step on blur/change for inputs whose value differs from before, so that filled-in form values appear as their own column?"

open question "Pixogram catalogue source of truth: should this extension bundle the PiX SVGs locally, or fetch the catalogue from a (versioned) PiX build artefact at install time? Bundling decouples the extension from PiX releases; fetching keeps icons in sync automatically. This question dissolves if both projects live in the same repository."

open question "user_cell suggestion heuristic: the recorder MAY seed user_cell. Which signals should suggest_user_cell consider — element kind alone (text_input -> pix-think), element kind plus form context (last click in a <form> -> pix-happy after a same-host nav), recent navigation history, page semantics from <meta name=description>? The exact heuristic is implementation-level but the inputs to it are domain-level and worth pinning down before shipping."

open question "PiX upstream id discipline: the bidirectional re-import relies on PiX preserving Score, Movement and ScoreStep ids end-to-end. Today PiX does not generate movement/step ids and its export strips the score id. Until that discipline ships in eadpucv/pix, UserImportsPiXScore can only fall back to positional matching. What is the migration path — patch PiX, vendor a fork, co-locate both projects in one repository?"
